{
  "step_1": "The decision reason evaluates skewness, specifically whether a lottery is left-skewed — meaning a greater portion of probability is assigned to favorable outcomes (i.e., higher monetary outcomes) compared to unfavorable ones (i.e., zero). In this case, both lotteries have a positive outcome (2400 or 2500 Euros) and a zero outcome. To assess skewness: a lottery is left-skewed if the probability mass is concentrated on the higher outcome (favorable) rather than the zero outcome (unfavorable). \n\nLottery A: 34% chance of 2400 Euros, 66% chance of 0 Euros → 34% on favorable, 66% on unfavorable → more probability on unfavorable → right-skewed.\nLottery B: 33% chance of 2500 Euros, 67% chance of 0 Euros → 33% on favorable, 67% on unfavorable → even more probability on unfavorable → also right-skewed.\n\nThus, neither lottery is left-skewed; both are right-skewed (more probability on zero). Therefore, the decision reason, which prefers the more left-skewed lottery, cannot be applied — because no lottery is left-skewed. The reason is not triggered in this case. However, the information is present in the lotteries and can be derived. So, the decision reason is applicable in terms of data availability, but the outcome (no left-skew) means it cannot be used to prefer one lottery over the other.",
  "step_2": "The verbal report states: 'höhere Wahrscheinlichkeit ist besser als nur 100 uro mehr Gewinn' (higher probability is better than only 100 euros more win). This indicates that the individual prioritizes the probability of winning (i.e., the chance of getting the prize) over the size of the prize. The individual is emphasizing that a higher probability (even if the prize is slightly smaller) is more valuable than a larger prize with a smaller probability. This reflects a preference for probability over expected value or prize size. The report does not mention skewness, left-skew, or any consideration of distribution shape. It focuses solely on probability as the deciding factor. The individual does not refer to the distribution of outcomes, nor does the individual compare the likelihood of favorable outcomes versus unfavorable ones in terms of skewness. Thus, the deliberation process centers on probability, not skewness.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason requires evaluating skewness — specifically, whether a lottery is more left-skewed (i.e., more probability on favorable outcomes). However, in this case, both lotteries have more than 50% probability on the zero outcome, meaning both are right-skewed. Therefore, neither is left-skewed, and the reason cannot identify a preference. The individual’s justification, however, is based on higher probability being better — which is unrelated to skewness. The individual does not mention skewness, distribution shape, or any comparison of favorable vs. unfavorable outcome probabilities. Therefore, the decision reason was not used. The individual’s reasoning is based on a different principle (probability preference), not skewness. There is no indication that the individual considered or applied the skewness rule. Thus, the decision reason was not used.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
}

{
